WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.387
By the end of this module, you should be able to explain when distributed processing is useful,

00:00:05.387 --> 00:00:12.107
describe the basic Spark execution model, and understand how Spark SQL and DataFrames connect.

00:00:12.107 --> 00:00:19.557
You should also be able to reason about lakehouse tables, schema choices, Delta tables, query plans,

00:00:19.557 --> 00:00:23.367
partitions, and shuffles at an introductory level.

00:00:23.367 --> 00:00:27.977
The goal is practical understanding, not cluster administration.

00:00:27.977 --> 00:00:32.717
Keep a real project in mind as we work through this section—for example,

00:00:32.717 --> 00:00:38.147
a retail pipeline aggregating high-volume sales data across partitions.

00:00:38.147 --> 00:00:43.477
The technical feature matters because it changes what questions we can answer reliably and how easily another

00:00:43.477 --> 00:00:47.062
analyst can understand our work.

00:00:47.062 --> 00:00:54.272
As you listen, try to connect each SQL or data concept to a decision you might need to

00:00:54.272 --> 00:00:57.667
defend later: what the data means, what one row represents, what can be missing,

00:00:57.667 --> 00:01:01.848
and what assumptions the transformation introduces.
